body:
![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The current implementation of `manifestDistinguishesSources` checks that the length of `entries` matches `REQUIRED_FIXTURE_IDS.length` and that every entry has a valid `fixture_id`. However, if `entries` contains duplicate entries for one ID and completely lacks another ID, this validation will erroneously pass. To prevent this silent failure and ensure the system fails closed, explicitly verify the presence and state of each expected element in `REQUIRED_FIXTURE_IDS`.

```suggestion
function manifestDistinguishesSources(manifest) {
  const entries = asArray(manifest?.fixture_manifest);
  const entryMap = new Map(entries.filter(isRecord).map((entry) => [entry.fixture_id, entry]));
  return REQUIRED_FIXTURE_IDS.every((id) => {
    const entry = entryMap.get(id);
    return entry
      && BASIS_KINDS.includes(entry.basis_kind)
      && Array.isArray(entry.covered_required_states)
      && entry.covered_required_states.length > 0
      && Array.isArray(entry.source_artifact_refs)
      && entry.source_artifact_refs.length > 0
      && (entry.basis_kind === 'synthetic_frontend_projection'
        ? entry.synthetic_frontend_projection === true
        : entry.synthetic_frontend_projection === false);
  }) && entries.length === REQUIRED_FIXTURE_IDS.length;
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When validating that an array contains a complete set of expected elements, explicitly verify the presence and state of each expected element rather than filtering and checking only the elements present in the array. Accompany this with regression tests that omit expected elements to ensure the validation fails closed.
</details>

