body:
![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Avoid using `JSON.stringify` for array comparisons in `wordingContractPresent`. Instead, use explicit element-by-element comparisons to ensure robust validation and avoid fragility associated with string serialization.

```javascript
function wordingContractPresent(fixtures) {
  return asArray(fixtures?.fixtures).every((fixture) => {
    const wording = asArray(fixture?.visible_wording);
    return wording.length === REQUIRED_VISIBLE_WORDING.length
      && wording.every((line, index) => line === REQUIRED_VISIBLE_WORDING[index]);
  });
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. Avoid using JSON.stringify for array comparisons in tests because it is strictly order-sensitive. Instead, use order-insensitive assertions, such as verifying the array length and checking element presence with includes or set-based checks.
</details>

