![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Using `JSON.stringify` for object comparison in `launchVerdictsPresent` is fragile because object key ordering is not guaranteed in JavaScript. Explicitly verify each expected key in the object to ensure robust validation.

```javascript
function launchVerdictsPresent(rehearsal, closeout) {
  const expected = launchReadinessVerdict();
  const keys = Object.keys(expected);
  return keys.every((key) => (
    rehearsal?.launch_readiness_verdict?.[key] === expected[key]
    && closeout?.launch_readiness_verdict?.[key] === expected[key]
  ));
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When validating that an object contains a complete set of required keys, explicitly verify against a list of expected keys rather than iterating only over the keys present in the object. Accompany this with regression tests that delete expected keys to ensure the validation fails closed.
</details>

